##TIME COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS

(i) Finding Maximum Element

for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)
{
    if (a[i] > max)
        max = a[i];
}

The loop runs from 1 to n-1.

Number of iterations = n - 1

Comparison is performed in every iteration.

T(n) = n - 1
     = O(n)

Therefore,

Worst-case Time Complexity = O(n)
Space Complexity = O(1)




(ii) Finding First and Second Largest Elements

for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)
{
    if (a[i] > first)
        ...
    else if (a[i] > second)
        ...
}

The loop runs from 1 to n-1.

Number of iterations = n - 1

Each iteration performs a constant number of comparisons.

T(n) = n - 1
     = O(n)

Therefore,

Worst-case Time Complexity = O(n)
Space Complexity = O(1)




(iii) Finding Mean

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
{
    sum += a[i];
}

The loop runs n times.

Number of iterations = n

T(n) = n
     = O(n)

Therefore,

Worst-case Time Complexity = O(n)
Space Complexity = O(1)






(iv) Finding Median

For finding the median of an unsorted array,
the array is first sorted using Bubble Sort.

Bubble Sort:

for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++)
{
    for (int j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++)
    {
        ...
    }
}

Outer loop runs = n - 1 times.

Inner loop runs approximately:
(n-1), (n-2), ..., 1 times.

Total comparisons:

T(n) = (n-1) + (n-2) + ... + 1

     = n(n-1)/2

     = (n^2 - n)/2

Ignoring constants and lower-order terms:

T(n) = O(n^2)

Finding the median after sorting takes O(1).

Therefore,

Worst-case Time Complexity = O(n^2)
Space Complexity = O(1)





(v) Finding Standard Deviation

The function performs two main operations.

First, finding the mean:

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    sum += a[i];

Time = O(n)

Second, calculating variance:

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
{
    sum += (a[i] - mean) * (a[i] - mean);
}

Time = O(n)

Total:

T(n) = O(n) + O(n)
     = O(2n)
     = O(n)

Therefore,

Worst-case Time Complexity = O(n)
Space Complexity = O(1)







(vi) Finding Mode

The function uses two nested loops.

Outer loop:

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)

Runs n times.

Inner loop:

for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)

Runs n times for each outer iteration.

Therefore:

T(n) = n × n
     = n^2

Hence,

Worst-case Time Complexity = O(n^2)
Space Complexity = O(1)





(vii) Removing All Duplicates

The function uses an outer loop and an inner loop.

Outer loop runs n times.

The inner loop checks previously stored elements.

In the worst case:

Comparisons = 0 + 1 + 2 + ... + (n-1)

              = n(n-1)/2

              = (n^2 - n)/2

Ignoring constants and lower-order terms:

T(n) = O(n^2)

Therefore,

Worst-case Time Complexity = O(n^2)
Space Complexity = O(n)


(viii) Reversing the Array

for (int i = 0; i < n/2; i++)
{
    swap elements
}

The loop runs n/2 times.

T(n) = n/2

Ignoring the constant 1/2:

T(n) = O(n)

Therefore,

Worst-case Time Complexity = O(n)
Space Complexity = O(1)


(ix) Partitioning the Array

The partition function uses two pointers:

i → moves from left to right
j → moves from right to left

Both pointers move through the array.

The total number of movements is at most proportional to n.

T(n) = O(n)

Therefore,

Worst-case Time Complexity = O(n)
Space Complexity = O(1)


 SUMMARY OF TIME AND SPACE COMPLEXITY

---------------------------------------------------------
Operation                         Time        Space
---------------------------------------------------------
(i) Maximum Element               O(n)        O(1)

(ii) First & Second Largest       O(n)        O(1)

(iii) Mean                        O(n)        O(1)

(iv) Median (Bubble Sort)         O(n^2)      O(1)

(v) Standard Deviation            O(n)        O(1)

(vi) Mode                         O(n^2)      O(1)

(vii) Remove Duplicates           O(n^2)      O(n)

(viii) Reverse Array              O(n)        O(1)

(ix) Partition Array              O(n)        O(1)
---------------------------------------------------------
